---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const regCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Register: the right key for the room', note: 'Same language, different keys: formal (job interview), casual (friends), professional jargon (workplace), intimate (family). Choosing the wrong register — too stiff or too street — signals outsider status faster than grammar errors do.' },
    { math: '2 · Idiom: the non-literal layer', note: '"Kick the bucket", "piece of cake", "spill the beans" — idioms are fixed phrases whose meanings can\'t be assembled from parts. They must be learned as whole units (chunks — the grammar lesson\'s logic), and they\'re everywhere: an estimated 25,000+ idioms in English.' },
    { math: '3 · Slang: the moving frontier', note: 'Slang marks in-group identity and changes fastest (generational turnover). The foreigner\'s dilemma: using slang wrongly reads as trying too hard; using none reads as distant. Learn to RECOGNISE slang before producing it.' },
    { math: '4 · Humor is the final exam', note: 'Puns, irony and sarcasm depend on pragmatics (the linguistics lesson) plus cultural knowledge. When you can make a native speaker laugh — deliberately — you\'ve crossed into real sociolinguistic competence.' },
  ],
};

const risDialsMatch = {
  "prompt": "The three dials of situation — pair each with the feature it selects:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "field — what is being talked about",
      "right": "vocabulary and jargon"
    },
    {
      "left": "tenor — the relationship",
      "right": "politeness, formality, the T–V pronoun choice"
    },
    {
      "left": "mode — the channel",
      "right": "density and structure: writing nominalises, speech hesitates"
    },
    {
      "left": "a mis-keyed message",
      "right": "outsider status, signalled faster than any grammar slip"
    }
  ],
  "why": "One message, four keys: the professor email, the friend text, the workplace channel and the partner note were all grammatical — the dial, not the grammar, is what natives use to place each other."
};

const risMarkerBuckets = {
  "prompt": "As formality rises, features climb, fall or track the relationship — file each:",
  "buckets": [
    "rises with formality",
    "falls as formality rises",
    "tracks tenor, not formality"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "\"the implementation of the policy\" instead of \"when they put the plan in place\"",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "nominalisation packing processes into analysable things",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "contractions — \"cannot\" becoming \"can't\"",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "fillers and casual discourse markers: \"like\", \"you know\"",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "hedging: \"might\", \"perhaps\", \"I was wondering\"",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "the T–V choice: tu/vous, du/Sie, tú/usted",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "office jargon as compression and membership badge: \"let's circle back\"",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "Density and nominalisation climb; contractions and fillers drop; hedging and pronoun choice encode social distance — and the register-switching drill (say it to a judge, a friend and a five-year-old) makes your own dial settings visible."
};

const risChunkEstimate = {
  "prompt": "What share of real spoken and written discourse is prefabricated chunks rather than novel combinations?",
  "unit": "% of text",
  "answer": 50,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "About half: natives sound fast because they assemble from ready-made blocks — which is why memorising high-frequency chunks beats generating word by word, and why idioms must be learned whole, with their social settings attached.",
  "context": "Deploy \"kick the bucket\" in a condolence message and you have an error no grammar checker would catch."
};

const risIdiomSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this summary is the vocabulary-myth speaking — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Idioms are frozen forms: \"kick the bucket\", never \"kick the bucket violently, twice by John\".",
    "A large vocabulary is enough to work any idiom out from its words.",
    "\"Kick the bucket\" is informal and faintly comic — a condolence-message hazard.",
    "Word-for-word rendering yields nonsense: Dutch \"it rains old women and pikesticks\".",
    "Idioms learned from shows and conversations arrive with frequency and social setting pre-installed."
  ],
  "errorLine": 1,
  "correction": "Frozen means the sense cannot be assembled from the parts — quasi-transparent ones are the most dangerous precisely because you think you follow them.",
  "why": "Chunk inventory, not word count, drives fluency — and idioms from lists arrive without the social settings that decide whether they fit."
};

const risSlangBranch = {
  "root": "You are out with people your age in the target-language city. Someone drops a slang item you have heard exactly once, three months ago. You want to belong. What do you do with that item?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "The item is current, in-group, and you are not sure of its edges. First decision:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Deploy it immediately — belonging demands production",
          "next": "bet",
          "feedback": "Production is a status bet: misfired slang reads as trying too hard, and a misfire is remembered."
        },
        {
          "label": "Bank it for recognition, keep producing neutral choices",
          "next": "check",
          "feedback": "Comprehension is risk-free and always useful — the asymmetry rule at work."
        },
        {
          "label": "Steer clear of slang altogether tonight",
          "next": "distant",
          "feedback": "Total avoidance has a cost too: it reads as distant or stiff."
        }
      ]
    },
    "bet": {
      "text": "You used it. It landed oddly — a pause, a half-smile. What happened?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "You broke a context boundary you could not see",
          "next": "lesson",
          "feedback": "Slang is context-bound: office, school and internet varieties barely overlap, and one item does not transfer across them."
        }
      ]
    },
    "check": {
      "text": "When would producing it become the right call?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Once you have heard speakers of your own age-and-context profile use it",
          "next": "lesson",
          "feedback": "Exactly — the subscription rule: only current, in-the-wild input keeps an item working."
        },
        {
          "label": "Once a dictionary lists it",
          "next": "lesson",
          "feedback": "Too slow: by the time a dictionary prints a term, its young users may have moved on."
        }
      ]
    },
    "distant": {
      "text": "You stayed silent and formal all evening.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Reconsider the middle path",
          "next": "check",
          "feedback": "Recognise everything, produce selectively — the symmetric dilemma has a known exit."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson": {
      "text": "Recognise everything, produce selectively — and only items you have heard from speakers of your own age-and-context profile.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "The dilemma is symmetrical — wrong slang tries too hard, no slang reads distant — and recognition is risk-free while production is a bet; verlan (meuf from femme) and internet coinages turn over too fast for memorised lists."
};

const risPoliteCompare = {
  "prompt": "One face-threat, two softening sites — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "English: soften with hedges and questions",
    "text": "\"I was wondering whether...\" is not a question about wondering — indirectness scales with social distance and power."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Japanese: build respect into the grammar",
    "text": "Whole grammatical systems of honorifics carry the face-work; requests wrap themselves in negatives and conditionals to preserve the other's option to refuse."
  },
  "why": "German directness reads as rude to a hedging culture and honest to itself — and pragmatic transfer is the C1 learner's most persistent residue: a grammatically perfect request imported from your first language's system can still strike locals as curt or obsequious."
};

const risCohesionMatch = {
  "prompt": "The C1 essay's connective tissue — pair each mechanism with its example:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "reference",
      "right": "this, such, the latter — pointing back without repeating"
    },
    {
      "left": "substitution and ellipsis",
      "right": "\"one more than last time\""
    },
    {
      "left": "conjunction",
      "right": "however, consequently, by contrast"
    },
    {
      "left": "lexical chains",
      "right": "the policy — the reform — the legislation"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Weak: four standalone sentences about a factory closure, none pointing anywhere. Strong: one sentence — \"The factory — closed suddenly, without consultation — took 400 jobs with it; for a town of 9,000, the closure was less an event than a change of identity\" — carrying every device at once."
};

const risRefusalPredict = {
  "q": "A request to a Japanese counterpart draws the answer: \"that would be a bit difficult.\" What has most likely arrived?",
  "choices": [
    "A maybe — push gently and it will become a yes",
    "A clear no, carried by an apology plus \"it's difficult\"",
    "A scheduling question about next week"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "East Asian norms often avoid a direct \"no\" — the apology-plus-difficulty formula carries the refusal, which a literal-minded learner hears as a maybe. The American \"we'll see\", meanwhile, is often a no where a German hears a schedule."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/register-idiom-and-slang">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the advanced Languages lesson where "correct" meets "appropriate." It assumes the
    ladder beneath it: the foundational <em>How to learn any language</em>, and from the
    intermediate tier <em>Grammar without tears</em> (you can already build correct
    sentences), <em>Your first 1000 words</em> and <em>Listening: comprehensible input</em>
    (your input engine), plus <em>Speaking and shadowing</em>. Those lessons built the
    sentence system; this page adds the social layer they deliberately left out.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>analyse any exchange into field, tenor and mode, and name the markers you would change;</li>
    <li>explain why fluency is largely chunk retrieval, and how to learn idioms with their social settings attached;</li>
    <li>apply the recognise-selectively-produce rule to slang, and justify it;</li>
    <li>perform requests, refusals and apologies in the target culture's own formulas and conditions;</li>
    <li>audit your own output with four measurable numbers: density, hedging, chunk ratio, register consistency.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The C1 level is where textbook competence meets the social layer. You can already construct correct
    sentences; natives produce <em>appropriate</em> ones — and appropriateness is a separate knowledge
    system: <strong>registers</strong> (which key the room is in), <strong>idioms and chunks</strong> (the
    prefabricated units real speech is largely made of), <strong>slang</strong> (the in-group frontier),
    and the pragmatic artistry beneath humour and politeness. This lesson maps each layer with the theory
    that explains it, and turns the CEFR's C1 descriptors — implicit meaning, spontaneous fluency, flexible
    social/academic/professional use, well-structured text — into concrete skills.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="register, idiom, slang, humour — the four social layers" widget="equationStep" config={regCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards and note that each layer is
    a different kind of knowledge: register is calibration, idiom is inventory, slang is
    membership, humour is integration. The order is also a difficulty ladder — the lower
    cards are learnable by study, the top one mostly by long immersion.
  </p>

  <h2>Register: the right key for the room</h2>
  <p>
    M. A. K. Halliday's register theory decomposes "situation" into three dials, each with its own linguistic
    reflexes: <strong>field</strong> (what is being talked about — it selects vocabulary and jargon),
    <strong>tenor</strong> (the relationship — it selects politeness, formality, pronoun choice), and
    <strong>mode</strong> (the channel — it selects density and structure: writing nominalises, speech
    hesitates). Worked example — one message, four keys. Email to a professor: "I was wondering whether you
    might have time to look at my draft at your convenience?" Text to a friend: "did u get my draft?? need it
    checked when you get a sec". Workplace channel: "Could you review the draft before Thursday? Happy to
    walk through comments." To a partner: "any chance you can look at my thing tonight??" All grammatical; a
    register error — the "dear professor, gimme a look" key mismatch — signals outsider status faster than
    any grammar slip, because register choice is exactly what natives use to place each other.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each dial with the linguistic reflex it selects" widget="match" config={risDialsMatch} />
  <p>
    The learnable markers are concrete. <strong>Lexical density and nominalisation</strong> rise with
    formality (write "the implementation of the policy" rather than "when they put the plan in place");
    <strong>contractions and fillers</strong> fall ("cannot" vs "can't," "like" and "you know" as casual
    discourse markers); <strong>modal softening</strong> (hedging: "might, perhaps, I was wondering") tracks
    politeness distance; <strong>pronoun systems</strong> encode tenor directly — the T–V choice (tu/vous,
    du/Sie, tú/usted) is register made grammatical, and choosing wrongly is a social act, not a typo.
    Professional registers add jargon as compression and membership badge: "let's circle back" identifies
    the office tribe. The training method is register-switching: say the same content to a judge, a friend
    and a five-year-old, then compare what you changed — you are learning your own register dial's settings
    in the new language.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each feature by its direction of travel" widget="sort-buckets" config={risMarkerBuckets} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "formal language is better language, so learners should
  always sound polite and careful." Textbooks teach the formal register first and exams
  reward it, so the belief is understandable. But register is a dial, not a ranking: "did u
  get my draft??" is the correct choice between friends, and the failure mode runs both ways
  — too stiff in the chat, too loose in the email. The skill natives use to place each other
  is matching the room, not maximising formality.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> register is a set of dials — field, tenor, mode — with concrete,
    trainable linguistic reflexes. The next layer explains why natives sound fast: much of
    their speech is not built at all, but retrieved.
  </p>

  <h2>Idiom: language is mostly prefabricated</h2>
  <p>
    The idiom principle (Sinclair's term) is the discovery that changed how we teach fluency:
    <strong>a large share of real discourse is prefabricated chunks</strong>, not novel combinations —
    Erman and Warren's corpus estimate puts it around half of spoken and written text. Idioms proper ("kick
    the bucket," "piece of cake," "spill the beans") are the extreme case: fixed forms whose meanings cannot
    be assembled from their parts and whose grammar is frozen ("kick the bucket" but not "kick the bucket
    violently, twice by John"). Around them sit the everyday chunks that make speech fast: collocations
    ("heavy rain," not "strong rain"), multi-word verbs, speech formulas ("I was wondering if..."), discourse
    routines. Two consequences for the learner. First, <strong>fluency is chunk retrieval</strong>: natives
    sound fast because they assemble from ready-made blocks — which is why memorising high-frequency chunks
    beats generating word by word. Second, <strong>idioms must be learned as units with their social
    settings attached</strong>: "kick the bucket" is informal and faintly comic — deploy it in a condolence
    message and you have an error no grammar checker would catch. Idioms learned from lists are misused
    hilariously; learned from shows and conversations they arrive with register and frequency pre-installed.
    Note also cross-language traps: many idioms translate word for word into nonsense ("it rains old women
    and pikesticks," Dutch regent oude wijven en pikestokken — the same idea, untranslatable form), and
    quasi-transparent idioms are the most dangerous, because you think you understand them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet on the chunk share of real discourse" widget="estimate" config={risChunkEstimate} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "idioms can be worked out from their words, so a big
  vocabulary is enough." Quasi-transparent idioms make this seductive — you feel you
  understand them. But idioms are frozen units whose meanings cannot be assembled from their
  parts ("kick the bucket" is about dying, not buckets) and whose grammar will not flex
  ("kick the bucket" but not "kick the bucket violently, twice by John"). Word-for-word
  translation across languages yields nonsense, and idioms learned from lists arrive without
  the register settings that decide whether they fit.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the line the vocabulary-myth would love" widget="spot-error" config={risIdiomSpot} />

  <h2>Slang: the moving frontier of belonging</h2>
  <p>
    Slang is sociolinguistics in motion: vocabulary marking in-group identity, generational membership and
    resistance to standard prestige — the <strong>covert prestige</strong> this ladder's sociolinguistics
    lesson formalised. It changes fastest of all layers (by the time a dictionary prints a slang term, its
    young-native users may have moved on), it is context-bound (office slang, school slang, internet slang
    barely overlap), and it is sociolectally specific — French verlan inverts syllables (meuf from femme),
    London's Multicultural English reshapes grammar, internet registers coin and retire words in months.
    The learner's dilemma is real and symmetrical: use slang wrongly and you read as trying too hard (the
    native equivalent of a dad saying "yeet" with full stops); use none and you read as distant or stiff.
    The professional strategy: <strong>recognise everything, produce selectively</strong> — comprehension
    (a C1 descriptor) is risk-free, production is a status bet you should make only when you have heard the
    specific item used by speakers of your own age-and-context profile. Slang is a subscription, not a
    download: only current, in-the-wild input keeps it working.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="run the slang decision in real time" widget="branch" config={risSlangBranch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "using slang makes you sound native, so a C1 learner
  should adopt it early and often." It seems reasonable because slang marks in-group
  belonging. But the dilemma is symmetrical — wrong slang reads as trying too hard, and
  slang turns over so fast that a memorised list is out of date before it is mastered. The
  section's asymmetry rule is the correct picture: recognise everything, produce selectively,
  and only items you have heard from speakers of your own age-and-context profile.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> register chooses the key, idiom supplies the prefabricated
    blocks, and slang marks who belongs. Beneath all three runs the layer that decides what
    the words actually do.
  </p>

  <h2>Pragmatics: politeness, irony, and the final exam</h2>
  <p>
    Beneath all three layers runs pragmatics. Brown and Levinson's politeness theory explains what register
    intuitions do: <strong>face-threats</strong> (requests, refusals, criticism) are softened in proportion
    to social distance and power — hence indirectness ("I was wondering whether..." is not a question about
    wondering) — and <em>where the softening happens varies by language</em> (English hedges and questions;
    Japanese builds whole grammatical systems of respect; German directness reads as rude to a hedging
    culture and honest to itself). Pragmatic transfer is the C1 learner's most persistent residue: a
    grammatically perfect request, imported from your first language's politeness system, can still strike
    natives as curt or obsequious. Then the art forms: <strong>irony and sarcasm</strong> require detecting
    the gap between literal content and intended meaning (Grice's implicature, fired with intent to amuse);
    <strong>humour</strong> requires cultural inventory — puns live on sound, irony on shared expectations,
    in-jokes on shared history. The CEFR's "recognise implicit meaning" descriptor is precisely this: reading
    what was not said. And the reliable milestone stands: when you can make a native speaker laugh
    <em>deliberately</em> — not with you, at you — you have crossed into genuine sociolinguistic competence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="drag the divider — two softening sites for one face-threat" widget="compare" config={risPoliteCompare} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> pragmatics explained indirectness, irony and the cross-cultural
    speech-act catalogue. The remaining C1 descriptors concern text — and cohesion, it turns
    out, can be built mechanically.
  </p>

  <h2>The C1 writing layer: cohesion made visible</h2>
  <p>
    The CEFR's final C1 descriptor — "clear, well-structured, detailed text... showing controlled use of
    organisational patterns, connectors and cohesive devices" — is a writing skill, and it can be built
    mechanically. Cohesion is achieved by four devices: <strong>reference</strong> (this, such, the
    latter — pointing back without repeating), <strong>substitution and ellipsis</strong> (one more than
    last time), <strong>conjunction</strong> (however, consequently, by contrast), and
    <strong>lexical cohesion</strong> (chains of related words: the policy — the reform — the
    legislation). Worked contrast. Weak: "The factory closed. The factory's workers lost jobs. The
    closure was decided suddenly. The jobs were important." Every sentence standalone, none pointing
    anywhere. Strong: "The factory — closed suddenly, without consultation — took 400 jobs with it; for
    a town of 9,000, the closure was less an event than a change of identity." One sentence carries the
    four devices and the analysis. Academic register adds its own layer on top:
    <strong>hedging</strong> calibrated to evidence ("suggests," "appears to," "may indicate" — never
    decorative, always a claim about strength of inference), <strong>nominalisation</strong> to pack
    processes into analysable things ("Prices rose because demand increased" becomes "the price rise
    reflects demand growth"), and <strong>reporting frames</strong> that position sources ("Smith
    concedes that..." is an argument, not a citation). These are trainable, examinable, and exactly what
    C1 assessment rewards — prose that argues, not just reports.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each connective device with its example" widget="match" config={risCohesionMatch} />

  <h2>Speech acts across cultures: the pragmatics catalogue, worked</h2>
  <p>
    The politeness layer becomes concrete when you compare how languages perform the same act.
    <strong>Requests</strong>: English prefers interrogatives with hedges ("Would you mind opening the
    window?"); German tolerates imperatives from strangers ("Mach bitte das Fenster auf" is friendly);
    Japanese wraps the request in negatives and conditionals to preserve the other's option to refuse.
    <strong>Refusals</strong>: East Asian norms often avoid a direct "no" (an apology plus "it's
    difficult" carries the refusal), so a Japanese "that would be a bit difficult" is a clear no that a
    literal-minded learner will hear as a maybe; an American "we'll see" often means no where a German
    hears a schedule. <strong>Compliments and apologies</strong> diverge just as sharply (who deflects,
    who accepts, whose fault-grammar requires the other party's repair). The training consequence is
    precise: learn each speech act in your target language as a <em>formulæ-plus-conditions</em> package
    — which formulas, at which social distance, with what face-work — because transferring your native
    pragmatics produces grammar-perfect rudeness or obsequiousness that no textbook flags. This is the
    deepest layer of "sounding native": not accent, but correctly calibrated illocutionary force.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet on what the refusal really said" widget="predict" config={risRefusalPredict} />

  <h2>A register audit you can run tonight</h2>
  <p>
    Make the theory measurable on your own output. Collect three texts you produced in the target
    language this month — one formal (an email), one casual (a chat message), one spoken-ish (a voice
    note transcript) — and audit them against the dial settings above. Count <strong>contractions</strong>
    per hundred words (formal writing in English: near zero; casual chat: most auxiliaries contracted);
    count <strong>hedging devices</strong> and see whether they correlate with social distance as
    predicted; list every <strong>chunk</strong> you used without constructing it ("as far as I know,"
    "on the other hand") and notice how much of the fluent 20% of your text was prefabricated; find one
    place where register slipped (the exclamation mark in the formal email, the missing softener in the
    request) and rewrite it. Then repeat the audit on a native speaker's comparable texts — the gap
    between the two profiles <em>is</em> your remaining C1 gap, itemised. The exercise converts "sounding
    native" from a vibe into a checklist: density, hedging, chunk ratio, register consistency — four
    numbers you can move deliberately, the way the financial-modelling lesson converts "the business is
    fine" into ratios.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three register dials and one linguistic reflex of each.</li>
    <li>A friend texts "lol same" in the target language and you reply with a full formal sentence. What exactly went wrong, and why would no grammar checker catch it?</li>
    <li>Why does the lesson recommend comprehending slang before producing it, rather than the reverse?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) field selects vocabulary and jargon; tenor selects
    politeness, formality and pronoun choice; mode selects density and structure — writing
    nominalises, speech hesitates. (2) The register key is wrong for the room: too stiff for
    casual chat, which signals outsider status faster than a grammar slip. A grammar checker
    sees only correct sentences; appropriateness is a separate knowledge system. (3)
    Comprehension is risk-free and always useful; production is a status bet that misfires
    unless the item matches your age-and-context profile — and a misfired slang item is
    remembered, while an unproduced one costs nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Register is three dials — field, tenor, mode — with learnable linguistic markers.</li>
    <li>Roughly half of real discourse is prefabricated chunks; fluency is chunk retrieval.</li>
    <li>Idioms are frozen units: learn them whole, with their social settings attached.</li>
    <li>Slang: recognise everything, produce selectively — a subscription, not a download.</li>
    <li>Politeness systems differ by language; transferring yours produces grammar-perfect rudeness.</li>
    <li>Making a native laugh deliberately is the reliable milestone of sociolinguistic competence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The subject's other advanced lessons push this one in two directions: <em>Translation
    versus interpretation</em> forces the register and idiom layers into another language's
    constraints in real time, and <em>Writing systems of the world</em> examines the
    technology underneath the literacy your C1 writing assumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three research threads deepen the picture. <strong>Corpus register analysis</strong>: Douglas Biber's
    multidimensional studies (1988) quantified what intuition knows — speech and writing differ along
    dimensions (involved vs informational, narrative vs non-narrative), and registers are points in that
    space, which gives the learner a measurable target: where, on Biber's dimensions, does your desired
    register sit? <strong>Formulaic sequences in acquisition</strong>: research on "islands of reliability"
    shows learners' fluent stretches are exactly their chunk inventory, and that formulaic language carries
    much of perceived nativelikeness — pronunciation teachers now target chunks, not words. <strong>L2
    pragmatics</strong> is a maturing field with a sober finding: classroom instruction helps (explicit
    teaching of speech-act formulas beats exposure alone), but sociopragmatic judgement — when to deploy
    what — develops mainly through feedback and years. Open questions: are internet-native registers
    (meme grammar, emoji pragmatics, discourse markers like "so" at message starts) stabilising into
    genuine registers with their own rules? Can models of politeness transfer across cultures at all, or is
    "face" itself culture-bound? And does artificial conversational competence — chatbots that nail register
    — change what natives consider the marks of belonging? Whatever the answers, the C1 target is now
    explicit: not more grammar, but the right grammar for the room, delivered in the community's own
    prefabricated pieces, with the unsaid correctly unsaid.
  </p>

